To determine the winner of the game, we can use a strategy based on the number of piles and the minimum possible number of moves required to split piles. Given that the game is about showing that a pile of stones can be split into multiple piles, the crucial observation is the fact that a pile with an odd number of stones can always be split by the player who goes next, and a pile with an even number of stones cannot be split further. This is because splitting a pile means further enumerating its numbers (integers). Therefore, when all piles have at least 2 stones, players will split stones until all piles are either 1 or even. In this scenario, a player who is left to split an empty pile ends up losing because all consecutive numbers have already been broken.

